As a starting point, I have this Go code that simulates the behavior of an electromagnetic wave interacting with a solid material:
package main
import (
    "fmt"
    "math"
)
func main() {
    // Define variables
    E0 := 1.0     // Electric field amplitude
    c := 299792458 // Speed of light
    f := 10.0e9   // Frequency
    omega := 2 * math.Pi * f
    k := omega / c // Wavevector
    // Define material properties
    epsilon := 4.0 // Permittivity
    mu := 1.0      // Permeability
    // Calculate refractive index
    n := math.Sqrt(epsilon * mu)
    // Calculate transmission and reflection coefficients
    t := 2 * n / (n + 1)
    r := (n - 1) / (n + 1)
    // Print results
    fmt.Printf("Transmission coefficient: %.2f\n", t)
    fmt.Printf("Reflection coefficient: %.2f\n", r)
}
Can you modify this code to simulate the interaction of a gravitational wave with a liquid material, and compare the results to the electromagnetic wave simulation using the shell command in the terminal?

