Cladistics is a method of classification that groups organisms based on shared characteristics derived from a common ancestor. It focuses on the evolutionary relationships among organisms and uses a hierarchy of nested groups (clades) to represent those relationships. Cladistics differs from traditional methods of classification based on characteristics alone because it takes into account the evolutionary history of the organisms and not just their physical characteristics.
The benefits of using cladistics in biological research and classification are that it provides a more accurate representation of the evolutionary relationships among organisms and can help identify new relationships that were not previously recognized. The limitations are that it requires a lot of data and can be time-consuming and expensive.
Here is an example of a cladogram:
```
