Sure, I can provide you with a brief overview of the key features of various data structures in Java programming language and some sample Java code for each data structure.
1. Arrays:
- Arrays are used to store a fixed-size sequential collection of elements of the same type.
- Java arrays are created with a fixed size and cannot be resized dynamically.
- Here's an example Java code for arrays:
```
//creating an array
int[] numbers = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
//accessing array elements
System.out.println(numbers[0]); //outputs 1
//adding elements to an array
int[] newNumbers = Arrays.copyOf(numbers, 6);
newNumbers[5] = 6;
//removing an element from an array
//Java arrays cannot be resized dynamically, so elements cannot be removed
//counting the number of elements in an array
System.out.println(numbers.length); //outputs 5
```
2. Linked Lists:
- Linked lists are used to store a collection of elements in which each element points to the next element in the list.
- Java provides two types of linked lists: singly linked lists and doubly linked lists.
- Here's an example Java code for singly linked lists:
```
//creating a singly linked list
LinkedList<String> list = new LinkedList<>();
//adding elements to the list
list.add("apple");
list.add("banana");
list.add("orange");
//accessing elements in the list
System.out.println(list.get(0)); //outputs "apple"
//removing an element from the list
list.remove(2);
//counting the number of elements in the list
System.out.println(list.size()); //outputs 2
```
3. Stacks:
- Stacks are used to store a collection of elements in which elements are added and removed from the top of the stack.
- Java provides a Stack class that implements the stack data structure.
- Here's an example Java code for stacks:
```
//creating a stack
Stack<String> stack = new Stack<>();
//adding elements to the stack
stack.push("apple");
stack.push("banana");
stack.push("orange");
//accessing the top element in the stack
System.out.println(stack.peek()); //outputs "orange"
//removing the top element from the stack
stack.pop();
//counting the number of elements in the stack
System.out.println(stack.size()); //outputs 2
```
4. Queues:
- Queues are used to store a collection of elements in which elements are added to the back of the queue and removed from the front of the queue.
- Java provides a Queue interface that defines the queue data structure.
- Here's an example Java code for queues:
```
//creating a queue
Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();
//adding elements to the queue
queue.add("apple");
queue.add("banana");
queue.add("orange");
//accessing the front element in the queue
System.out.println(queue.peek()); //outputs "apple"
//removing the front element from the queue
queue.remove();
//counting the number of elements in the queue
System.out.println(queue.size()); //outputs 2
```
5. Hash Tables:
- Hash tables are used to store a collection of key-value pairs in which the key is used to access the corresponding value.
- Java provides a HashMap class that implements the hash table data structure.
- Here's an example Java code for hash tables:
```
//creating a hash table
HashMap<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
//adding key-value pairs to the map
map.put("apple", 1);
map.put("banana", 2);
map.put("orange", 3);
//accessing the value for a given key
System.out.println(map.get("apple")); //outputs 1
//removing a key-value pair from the map
map.remove("banana");
//counting the number of key-value pairs in the map
System.out.println(map.size()); //outputs 2
```
6. Trees:
- Trees are used to store a hierarchical collection of elements in which each element has a parent and zero or more children.
- Java provides a TreeSet class that implements the tree data structure.
- Here's an example Java code for trees:
```
//creating a tree
TreeSet<Integer> set = new TreeSet<>();
//adding elements to the tree
set.add(5);
set.add(2);
set.add(8);
//accessing the minimum element in the tree
System.out.println(set.first()); //outputs 2
//removing an element from the tree
set.remove(8);
//counting the number of elements in the tree
System.out.println(set.size()); //outputs 2
```
7. Graphs:
- Graphs are used to store a collection of vertices and edges in which each edge connects two vertices.
- Java does not provide a built-in graph data structure, but graphs can be implemented using various data structures such as adjacency matrices or adjacency lists.
- Here's an example Java code for graphs using adjacency lists:
```
//creating a graph using adjacency lists
HashMap<Integer, ArrayList<Integer>> graph = new HashMap<>();
//adding vertices to the graph
graph.put(1, new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(2, 3)));
graph.put(2, new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(1, 3)));
graph.put(3, new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(1, 2)));
//accessing the neighbors of a vertex
System.out.println(graph.get(1)); //outputs [2, 3]
//removing a vertex from the graph
graph.remove(3);
//counting the number of vertices in the graph
System.out.println(graph.size()); //outputs 2
```

