As a bonus challenge, can you provide a C++ code implementation of a sorting algorithm, such as QuickSort or MergeSort, and explain its time complexity and efficiency in different scenarios? 
[Specific C++ code]
For example, here's an implementation of QuickSort:
void quickSort(int arr[], int low, int high) {
    if (low < high) {
        int pivot = partition(arr, low, high);
        quickSort(arr, low, pivot - 1);
        quickSort(arr, pivot + 1, high);
    }
}
int partition(int arr[], int low, int high) {
    int pivot = arr[high];
    int i = (low - 1);
    for (int j = low; j <= high - 1; j++) {
        if (arr[j] < pivot) {
            i++;
            swap(&arr[i], &arr[j]);
        }
    }
    swap(&arr[i + 1], &arr[high]);
    return (i + 1);
}
The time complexity of QuickSort is O(nlogn) on average, but can degrade to O(n^2) in the worst case (when the pivot is chosen poorly). QuickSort is a popular choice for sorting large datasets, as it has good performance characteristics and is relatively easy to implement.

