Yes, I can provide you with a Java code that generates a secure and randomized password that meets the given requirements. Here's the code:
```java
import java.security.SecureRandom;
import java.util.Random;
public class PasswordGenerator {

    private static final String CHAR_LOWER = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz";
    private static final String CHAR_UPPER = CHAR_LOWER.toUpperCase();
    private static final String NUMBER = "0123456789";
    private static final String OTHER_CHAR = "!@#$%&*()_+-=[]?";
    private static final String PASSWORD_ALLOW_BASE = CHAR_LOWER + CHAR_UPPER + NUMBER + OTHER_CHAR;
    private static final int PASSWORD_LENGTH = 12;
    private static Random random = new SecureRandom();
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
            String password = generateRandomPassword();
            System.out.println("password = " + password);
        }
    }
    public static String generateRandomPassword() {
        StringBuilder password = new StringBuilder(PASSWORD_LENGTH);
        // pick characters randomly from the allowed base and add to password
        password.append(randomChar(CHAR_LOWER));
        password.append(randomChar(CHAR_UPPER));
        password.append(randomChar(NUMBER));
        password.append(randomChar(OTHER_CHAR));
        for (int i = 0; i < PASSWORD_LENGTH - 4; i++) {
            password.append(randomChar(PASSWORD_ALLOW_BASE));
        }
        // shuffle characters in password
        String shuffled = shuffleString(password.toString());
        return shuffled;
    }
    private static char randomChar(String characterSet) {
        int randomIndex = random.nextInt(characterSet.length());
        return characterSet.charAt(randomIndex);
    }
    private static String shuffleString(String string) {
        char[] passwordArray = string.toCharArray();
        for (int i = 0; i < passwordArray.length; i++) {
            int randomIndex = random.nextInt(passwordArray.length);
            char temp = passwordArray[i];
            passwordArray[i] = passwordArray[randomIndex];
            passwordArray[randomIndex] = temp;
        }
        return new String(passwordArray);
    }
}
```
This code generates a password of length 12 that contains at least one uppercase letter, one lowercase letter, one number, and one special character. The password is generated securely using the `SecureRandom` class in Java.

