How can I modify the given C++ code to generate all possible combinations of subjects, verbs, and objects in a list without repeating any combination? The list of subjects, verbs, and objects are:
Subjects: ["Explorer", "Captain", "Adventurer", "Navigator", "Pioneer"]
Verbs: ["discovered", "charted", "explored", "navigated", "braved"]
Objects: ["unchartered waters", "new land", "hidden treasures", "uncharted territories", "unknown dangers"]
Here is the given C++ code:
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    vector<string> subjects = {"Explorer", "Captain", "Adventurer", "Navigator", "Pioneer"};
    vector<string> verbs = {"discovered", "charted", "explored", "navigated", "braved"};
    vector<string> objects = {"unchartered waters", "new land", "hidden treasures", "uncharted territories", "unknown dangers"};
    vector<vector<string>> stories;

    // Your code here

    int numberOfStories = stories.size();
    cout << "I can generate " << numberOfStories << " unique and creative stories using this list." << endl;
    return 0;
}

output:
Here's the modified C++ code that generates all possible combinations of subjects, verbs, and objects in a list without repeating any combination:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    vector<string> subjects = {"Explorer", "Captain", "Adventurer", "Navigator", "Pioneer"};
    vector<string> verbs = {"discovered", "charted", "explored", "navigated", "braved"};
    vector<string> objects = {"unchartered waters", "new land", "hidden treasures", "uncharted territories", "unknown dangers"};
    vector<vector<string>> stories;
    // Generate all possible combinations
    for (auto subject : subjects) {
        for (auto verb : verbs) {
            for (auto object : objects) {
                vector<string> story = {subject, verb, object};
                // Check if the combination already exists in the stories vector
                if (find(stories.begin(), stories.end(), story) == stories.end()) {
                    stories.push_back(story);
                }
            }
        }
    }
    int numberOfStories = stories.size();
    cout << "I can generate " << numberOfStories << " unique and creative stories using this list." << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
In this modified code, we use three nested loops to generate all possible combinations of subjects, verbs, and objects. We create a vector called `story` that contains the current combination of subject, verb, and object, and then we check if this combination already exists in the `stories` vector using the `find()` function. If the combination is not found in the `stories` vector, we add it to the vector using the `push_back()` function.
Finally, we print the number of unique stories that have been generated.

